WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:06.880
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome to 8.701.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.610
So in this lecture, we'll give
you the first introduction

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.020
to Feynman diagram.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:15.880
This is part 1 out of a few
sections on Feynman diagrams.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.010
So this is really meant to
introduce the topic such

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:21.130
that we can use the
same language to talk

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.770
about Feynman diagrams
before we then later on are

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.440
able to use them as
a tool to calculate

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.740
interesting processes.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.190
This brings me right
to the essence already.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:36.430
What is a Feynman diagrams
and what can it be used for?

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:39.250
They arise from
pertubative calculations

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.700
of amplitude for reactions.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.280
And that's exactly how we're
going to use them later on.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.310
It turns out that the
mathematical terms

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:54.670
in the perturbation series can
be represented as a diagram.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.380
And then you can
turn this around

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.800
and use the diagram in order
to perform a calculation.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.209
So each of the diagrams then
indicates a particular factor

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:05.319
in the calculation.

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:07.300
Again, and then you
have a rule which

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.790
allows you to,
after drawing, you

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.760
can then put the pieces
together in order

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.940
to perform [INAUDIBLE]
calculation.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:19.730
The derivation of
those tools or rules

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.090
is beyond the content
of this course.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.150
But I will teach you how
to actually use diagrams

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.660
in order to calculate things.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.780
So here's one
example of a diagram.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.110
Let me just put this down
here so you can see this.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:41.620
So this is an electron
radiating a photon.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:46.670
You see components
like those lines here.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:50.210
Those represent particles
with energy and momentum

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.150
also what to consider the spin.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:54.290
And they meet at a point.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:58.230
This point here is
called a vertex.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.820
And this is where the
interaction takes place.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.790
And in this example.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:06.315
The vertex is labeled
with a q or e,

00:02:06.315 --> 00:02:10.169
representing the charge, the
electric charge, which gives us

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:12.330
the strength of the coupling.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.670
We already discussed when
we talked about units

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:19.260
that we can express the strength
of the electromagnetic--

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.650
the coupling in QED with
the electric charge.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:23.960
And that's shown below again.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.210
The amplitude then turns out to
be proportional to the charge

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:32.890
or to this coupling.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:37.030
And the diagrams with n vertices
for n of those components

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:43.200
here get a factor e,
the charge, to the nth

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.020
power in the amplitude,
and e to the second.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.460
Because if we're going to
calculate a probability,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.730
you have to square
the amplitude.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:55.030
You get a factor of e to 2n.

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:58.440
Again, don't get
confused-- e is charge.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.430
So for n vertices, there will
be a factor alpha to the nth

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.130
power for the probability.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:10.690
And so since alpha
is 1/137, you see

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.020
that if I want to
do a calculation,

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:19.180
and diagrams which have n
vertices will be suppressed,

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.350
will not contribute
much to our perturbation

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:24.860
serious because alpha is
much, much smaller than 1.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.550
So this is already an
interesting finding.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.690
Can restrict yourself to
calculating diagrams which

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:35.200
have a couple of
vertices or n vertices,

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.840
but you don't have to
calculate the entire series.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.340
You want to measure
your calculation

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.465
with experimental findings.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.560
Interesting here--
antiparticles.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.770
If you have a specific
vertex and you calculated it,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.000
it can be reused.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.730
It can be reused for
example by replacing

00:03:53.730 --> 00:04:01.200
a particle with an
antiparticle or by re-labeling.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.600
One thing I haven't
explained to you yet,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.340
you have to define
when you write them

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.790
which is the direction of time.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:10.330
We'll come to this direction.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:14.070
And so in this
case here, you have

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:19.839
a particle and an antiparticle
unrelating to a photon.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.850
So far, so good so.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:29.600
This is again a good
point to stop and just

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.770
try to read the diagrams.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:35.960
Note that what happens in this
discussion when you actually

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.540
change the direction of time--

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:39.440
forward down.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.230
You want either directions.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:47.030
So now if you want to
calculate the reaction,

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.920
it's not sufficient to
just use one word vertex.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:50.420
Why?

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:53.165
Because a single vertex will not
be able to give us a reaction.

00:04:53.165 --> 00:04:54.710
You can simply see
this when you look

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:59.560
at something like an electron
plus electron photon.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.970
This is not really possible
because of energy and momentum

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:04.550
conservation in this diagram.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:08.230
So you need a couple of vertices
in order to make a reaction.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:12.090
So this here is, again,
we have potentially

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.440
the time going this direction.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.770
There's a scattering between
an electron and a muon

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.300
through the exchange
of a photon.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.570
Both particles have
electric charge of e.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.660
And then you can
just calculate what

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:29.100
is the probability for a
process like this to occur.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.670
We'll see how to do this
technically later on.

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:34.040
But hopefully you have
a first impression.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:36.290
Again, let's label
this now very quickly.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:38.365
So you have an
incoming particle,

00:05:38.365 --> 00:05:41.900
a second incoming particle,
outgoing particles,

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.510
and an exchange particle.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.890
So this exchange
particle is a photon.

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:50.860
And there's two vertices
in this diagram.

